credd-15 -> cred    kind    server                      comment
credd-15 -> gl      gitlab  https://gitlab.example.com
credd-15 -> k2      sshkey                              brought in
credd-15 -> gli-mr  link                                what the review bot asks for
# exit credd-15 code:0
# start credd-16 cmd:"$bindir/credd -renew:k2 -src:@$tempdir/tok"
# eof credd-16
credd-16 -> credd.error  credd.notoken  cred:k2  comment:"an ssh key is not renewed; -keygen a new one and -remove this"
# exit credd-16 code:1
# start credd-17 cmd:"$bindir/credd -renew:gli-mr -expires:2027-01-31"
credd-17 <- glpat-renewed
# eof credd-17
credd-17 -> gli-mr is a link to gl, so that is the token renewed.
credd-17 -> Renewing gl, a gitlab token for me at https://gitlab.example.com.
credd-17 -> The links gli-mr stand for it and follow along.
credd-17 -> It was issued with these permissions, which the new token needs too:
credd-17 ->   api: Full read and write access to the API, including all groups and projects, the registry and package registry
credd-17 -> Sign in to https://gitlab.example.com as me and open https://gitlab.example.com/-/user_settings/personal_access_tokens?name=gl&scopes=api, which is the access-tokens page with the name gl and the scopes api filled in. Set an expiration, then Create personal access token. Copy the token; GitLab shows it once. Paste it below. Revoke the old token on the same page once the new one works.
credd-17 -> The new value replaces the old one in the store once you paste it and give the master password; the running daemon serves it at once.
credd-17 -> credd.renewed  cred:gl  expires:2027-01-31
# exit credd-17 code:0
# unsetenv CREDD_PASSWORD
# start credd-18 cmd:"$bindir/credd -show:gl"
# eof credd-18
credd-18 -> glpat-renewed
